WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.070 --> 00:00:05.049
Speaker 1: Today we're going to be speaking
with Garrett Bruhat, who's a Ph.D. candidate

00:00:05.049 --> 00:00:11.050
at the University of Rochester working at
the Laboratory of Laser Energetics, the largest

00:00:11.050 --> 00:00:15.200
university laser lab, and one of the only
places in the world that can do laser driven

00:00:15.200 --> 00:00:16.740
fusion experiments.

00:00:16.740 --> 00:00:18.700
Garrett, really great having you on today.

00:00:18.700 --> 00:00:19.700
I appreciate it.

00:00:19.700 --> 00:00:20.700
Speaker 3: Yeah, happy to.

00:00:20.700 --> 00:00:21.700
Come on.

00:00:21.700 --> 00:00:27.240
Speaker 1: David So I want to talk about this
recent breakthrough that's made a lot of news

00:00:27.240 --> 00:00:29.779
in the field of fusion energy.

00:00:29.779 --> 00:00:32.040
Before we even get to that.

00:00:32.040 --> 00:00:35.730
Let's maybe develop an understanding of what
we mean when we say fusion energy.

00:00:35.730 --> 00:00:38.880
I think for a lot of people, it's sort of
straightforward to understand, okay, you can

00:00:38.880 --> 00:00:44.720
take something like coal and you burn it and
then you can use that heat to do different

00:00:44.720 --> 00:00:45.720
things.

00:00:45.720 --> 00:00:47.000
I think many people can understand that.

00:00:47.000 --> 00:00:48.980
Or you burn gasoline in your car.

00:00:48.980 --> 00:00:50.730
When we're talking about fusion energy.

00:00:50.730 --> 00:00:51.820
What exactly is it?

00:00:51.820 --> 00:00:52.820
What are we talking about?

00:00:52.820 --> 00:00:53.820
What's the mechanism?

00:00:53.820 --> 00:00:54.820
Speaker 3: Yeah.

00:00:54.820 --> 00:01:00.550
So Fusion is one of the two primary forms
of nuclear energy that we can acquire right

00:01:00.550 --> 00:01:01.550
now.

00:01:01.550 --> 00:01:02.550
What?

00:01:02.550 --> 00:01:03.550
We do it all over the world.

00:01:03.550 --> 00:01:07.970
In fact, what's keeping my lights on right
now is fusion, where we split very heavy things

00:01:07.970 --> 00:01:13.360
like uranium and utilize the heat to boil
water, turn a turbine and make power to power

00:01:13.360 --> 00:01:15.090
our homes.

00:01:15.090 --> 00:01:17.400
Fusion is what powers the sun.

00:01:17.400 --> 00:01:20.880
It's also what powers our largest thermonuclear
weapons.

00:01:20.880 --> 00:01:25.610
It's where we take very small atoms like helium
and force them together.

00:01:25.610 --> 00:01:28.720
That generates an enormous amount of energy.

00:01:28.720 --> 00:01:36.159
And we have never been able to do a controlled,
sustained fusion chain reaction on Earth without

00:01:36.159 --> 00:01:39.720
a thermonuclear weapon until last week or
two weeks ago.

00:01:39.720 --> 00:01:47.719
Speaker 1: So fusion is the coming together
of atoms in order to produce.

00:01:47.719 --> 00:01:50.150
Is it is it accurate to say to produce heat?

00:01:50.150 --> 00:01:55.500
Speaker 3: It produces several forms of energy,
but everything will eventually just get converted

00:01:55.500 --> 00:01:59.119
to heat, to boil water or heat up gases or
something like that.

00:01:59.119 --> 00:02:03.549
It's it's kind of the the old adage that no
matter what humanity does, we eventually boil

00:02:03.549 --> 00:02:04.549
water.

00:02:04.549 --> 00:02:05.549
Speaker 1: Right.

00:02:05.549 --> 00:02:06.549
Okay.

00:02:06.549 --> 00:02:12.700
So in other words, fusion is the separation
of atoms, whereas fusion is the combination.

00:02:12.700 --> 00:02:15.470
That's the that's sort of the fundamental
difference we're talking about.

00:02:15.470 --> 00:02:16.580
Speaker 3: Yes.

00:02:16.580 --> 00:02:22.630
Speaker 1: So the breakthrough, which you
alluded to at Lawrence Livermore National

00:02:22.630 --> 00:02:29.330
Lab in California, it's being described as
a huge advancement towards nuclear fusion.

00:02:29.330 --> 00:02:31.580
What what exactly is it that happened?

00:02:31.580 --> 00:02:32.739
Speaker 3: Yeah.

00:02:32.739 --> 00:02:35.239
So it is a really big scientific achievement.

00:02:35.239 --> 00:02:36.680
It's a huge advancement.

00:02:36.680 --> 00:02:43.620
It's been the goal essentially ever since
we discovered fusion to get a fusion chain

00:02:43.620 --> 00:02:46.480
reaction in the laboratory.

00:02:46.480 --> 00:02:49.050
So humanity has generated fusion chain reactions.

00:02:49.050 --> 00:02:55.129
We did it first in the 1950s with the IT thermonuclear
weapon, and now it's some of our scariest

00:02:55.129 --> 00:02:56.129
weapons.

00:02:56.129 --> 00:02:57.129
But that is not useful.

00:02:57.129 --> 00:03:00.440
That is just a capability we have.

00:03:00.440 --> 00:03:08.150
We want fusion on a small scale controllable,
so that we can make clean sources of power.

00:03:08.150 --> 00:03:12.049
And that has been very, very challenging to
do in what Lawrence Livermore National Labs

00:03:12.049 --> 00:03:18.569
did, was they generated a self-sustained fusion
chain reaction in the lab no nuclear bombs,

00:03:18.569 --> 00:03:20.390
no gigantic destruction.

00:03:20.390 --> 00:03:23.519
Everything was controlled and done and is
repeatable.

00:03:23.519 --> 00:03:25.540
And that has never been done before.

00:03:25.540 --> 00:03:27.360
So it is it is a huge advancement.

00:03:27.360 --> 00:03:28.739
It's kind of like a Kitty Hawk moment.

00:03:28.739 --> 00:03:30.580
The airplane is finally taking off.

00:03:30.580 --> 00:03:37.100
Speaker 1: What would it look like to use
this technology to generate the energy that

00:03:37.100 --> 00:03:39.810
humans use for day to day lives?

00:03:39.810 --> 00:03:40.810
What would it do?

00:03:40.810 --> 00:03:43.319
We know what a fusion power plant would look
like.

00:03:43.319 --> 00:03:47.739
Speaker 3: Yeah, there's a lot of different
designs using lots of different technologies.

00:03:47.739 --> 00:03:53.010
There's a there's a huge plethora of ideas
for how to use fusion once you can get ignition,

00:03:53.010 --> 00:04:00.050
which is what Livermore did for for your average
person, it's going to look just like a fusion

00:04:00.050 --> 00:04:01.050
power plant.

00:04:01.050 --> 00:04:06.230
It'll be just a big chunk of concrete that
will make an enormous amount of power for

00:04:06.230 --> 00:04:08.239
the amount of land it takes up.

00:04:08.239 --> 00:04:13.060
There will be no CO2 and you might see some
clouds coming out from the cooling water and

00:04:13.060 --> 00:04:14.060
that would be about it.

00:04:14.060 --> 00:04:18.820
Speaker 1: So with with our traditional, is
it fair to say that our traditional nuclear

00:04:18.820 --> 00:04:22.380
power plants, these are what we would call
fission nuclear power plants?

00:04:22.380 --> 00:04:23.380
Yes.

00:04:23.380 --> 00:04:25.139
Speaker 3: They split atoms and thus they
do fission.

00:04:25.139 --> 00:04:27.139
Speaker 1: At those plants.

00:04:27.139 --> 00:04:34.699
One of the concerns that exists is what do
you do with the spent fuel and how long it

00:04:34.699 --> 00:04:35.699
lasts, etc..

00:04:35.699 --> 00:04:42.539
Now, I've said before I think that those who
are basing their opinion on nuclear fission

00:04:42.539 --> 00:04:48.530
based on 40 year old plants are probably not
accurately assessing them based on what modern

00:04:48.530 --> 00:04:49.960
plants would look like.

00:04:49.960 --> 00:04:55.169
But putting that issue aside for a second,
you still have this issue of the spent fuel

00:04:55.169 --> 00:04:58.289
in a fusion plant.

00:04:58.289 --> 00:04:59.710
What are the.

00:04:59.710 --> 00:05:02.840
Source materials and what's left over after
the fact.

00:05:02.840 --> 00:05:08.750
Speaker 3: So the source material is some
special forms of hydrogen, but it's something

00:05:08.750 --> 00:05:12.490
that's in the glass of water I'm drinking
from right now called deuterium.

00:05:12.490 --> 00:05:13.780
So that's heavy hydrogen.

00:05:13.780 --> 00:05:15.340
It's very common.

00:05:15.340 --> 00:05:16.340
It's not a big deal.

00:05:16.340 --> 00:05:22.320
It's used in all sorts of applications, primarily
nuclear, but even in medical tracers.

00:05:22.320 --> 00:05:26.180
And we have effectively an infinite supply
of it.

00:05:26.180 --> 00:05:31.010
It would, you know, the sun will be long burnt
out before we ever burn through the deuterium

00:05:31.010 --> 00:05:37.970
supply, the type of fusion that Lawrence Livermore
did, which is the quote unquote easiest fusion

00:05:37.970 --> 00:05:39.419
is still very, very hard.

00:05:39.419 --> 00:05:41.660
It's called deuterium, tritium.

00:05:41.660 --> 00:05:46.350
So the other input is a special type of radioactive
hydrogen called tritium.

00:05:46.350 --> 00:05:49.419
Well, we have to breed that via lithium.

00:05:49.419 --> 00:05:54.360
So from the perspective of an outside observer
ignoring all the science, the plant will eat

00:05:54.360 --> 00:05:59.370
deuterium, which we just get from water and
a small amount of lithium.

00:05:59.370 --> 00:06:04.190
Now we we get concerns about lithium supplies
because of electric cars and things like that.

00:06:04.190 --> 00:06:08.340
We're talking like 100 kilos of lithium a
year for a power plant that could power a

00:06:08.340 --> 00:06:09.340
major city.

00:06:09.340 --> 00:06:11.930
It's not a it's not a major concern.

00:06:11.930 --> 00:06:15.569
It's just like with vision, where the total
amount of material going in and out is just

00:06:15.569 --> 00:06:17.080
minuscule.

00:06:17.080 --> 00:06:19.590
There is no direct nuclear waste.

00:06:19.590 --> 00:06:22.639
There's no spent fuel from fusion.

00:06:22.639 --> 00:06:24.050
It just makes helium.

00:06:24.050 --> 00:06:29.461
But it also makes an absurd number of neutrons,
which is a type of radiation, and it will

00:06:29.461 --> 00:06:31.530
make the inside of the reactor radioactive.

00:06:31.530 --> 00:06:34.930
There is no way to get around radioactive
waste.

00:06:34.930 --> 00:06:39.180
Any time we do any kind of nuclear activities,
including medicine, every hospital generates

00:06:39.180 --> 00:06:41.199
a large flux of radioactive waste.

00:06:41.199 --> 00:06:42.199
But it's a benefit.

00:06:42.199 --> 00:06:46.860
It's cancer treatment and therapies and all
this good stuff.

00:06:46.860 --> 00:06:50.669
And we know how to handle the waste from these
sorts of systems.

00:06:50.669 --> 00:06:54.610
Fusion would not generate the very long lived,
spent fuel.

00:06:54.610 --> 00:06:58.110
As you pointed out, we can handle the spent
fuel from vision, but ignoring that fusion

00:06:58.110 --> 00:06:59.240
won't generate it.

00:06:59.240 --> 00:07:03.770
We're talking things more like 100 years of
radioactive lifetime, and there's ideas for

00:07:03.770 --> 00:07:07.169
how to even shorten that.

00:07:07.169 --> 00:07:10.979
So that that is one of the big advantages
that people point to with fusion.

00:07:10.979 --> 00:07:19.620
Speaker 1: What are the risks of this technology
applied as a power plant, for example, to

00:07:19.620 --> 00:07:21.200
power a city, as you mentioned?

00:07:21.200 --> 00:07:22.200
What can go wrong?

00:07:22.200 --> 00:07:24.699
And if something goes wrong, what happens.

00:07:24.699 --> 00:07:31.520
Speaker 3: In some sort of kind of absurd
worst case scenario, you could have radioactive

00:07:31.520 --> 00:07:33.800
material released to the environment.

00:07:33.800 --> 00:07:37.500
It would be small, it would be the impact
would be very low.

00:07:37.500 --> 00:07:41.200
The general issues would be things to people
at the plant.

00:07:41.200 --> 00:07:44.810
Very similar to what's happened with vision
failures.

00:07:44.810 --> 00:07:48.680
You if you worked at the plant, you'd be worried
you might have to fight a fire or something

00:07:48.680 --> 00:07:49.680
like that.

00:07:49.680 --> 00:07:51.139
Often fusion is in fusion.

00:07:51.139 --> 00:07:53.430
Power plants are envisioned to use very interesting,
weird coolant.

00:07:53.430 --> 00:07:57.180
So you imagine like flowing super hot liquid
lithium.

00:07:57.180 --> 00:07:58.860
I'm not so afraid of the radiation.

00:07:58.860 --> 00:08:02.889
I'm afraid of blowing liquid lithium getting
in the air because that will make a very nasty

00:08:02.889 --> 00:08:04.669
fire.

00:08:04.669 --> 00:08:08.949
But these are all you know, fusion is going
to be regulated just like vision.

00:08:08.949 --> 00:08:11.440
It's going to be very, very tightly controlled.

00:08:11.440 --> 00:08:15.259
And everyone takes this job very seriously
as to how it would be put together.

00:08:15.259 --> 00:08:19.150
I would I would be more than willing, just
like I'm willing to live right now, right

00:08:19.150 --> 00:08:20.449
next to efficient plant.

00:08:20.449 --> 00:08:24.139
I wouldn't have any concern living next to
a fusion plant right now.

00:08:24.139 --> 00:08:27.780
Speaker 1: If my understanding is still accurate.

00:08:27.780 --> 00:08:35.219
It takes somewhere between six and ten years
to get a new nuclear fusion plant up and running.

00:08:35.219 --> 00:08:37.800
What is the timeline?

00:08:37.800 --> 00:08:40.630
And maybe it's hard to say because we don't
yet exactly know what these would look like.

00:08:40.630 --> 00:08:44.959
But is there a different timeline for fusion
energy plants?

00:08:44.959 --> 00:08:49.680
Speaker 3: There is hope that it could be
sped up, but I honestly think the sort of

00:08:49.680 --> 00:08:53.589
things that would speed up a fusion plant
construction will speed up vision construction

00:08:53.589 --> 00:08:54.589
as well.

00:08:54.589 --> 00:08:58.950
A lot of the issues that we see with vision
come down to just some of the dumbest stuff,

00:08:58.950 --> 00:09:03.040
like pouring concrete and laying steel.

00:09:03.040 --> 00:09:07.800
Fusion requires a lot more complex equipment,
too, so it's going to be many iterations of

00:09:07.800 --> 00:09:12.959
learning how to build these power plants before
we ever get good at it.

00:09:12.959 --> 00:09:17.100
It's not something I would expect to see even
even if everything was ready to go tomorrow,

00:09:17.100 --> 00:09:22.300
I wouldn't expect to see power plants just
going up left and right without massive, honestly

00:09:22.300 --> 00:09:23.300
government support.

00:09:23.300 --> 00:09:26.800
You have to look at how places like France
did their large vision built out.

00:09:26.800 --> 00:09:30.339
So you just have to throw tons of money and
personnel at it.

00:09:30.339 --> 00:09:37.579
Speaker 1: When you look at something like
let me put it a different way, when you talk

00:09:37.579 --> 00:09:44.450
about this being difficult, fusion being difficult,
what does that really mean in the sense that

00:09:44.450 --> 00:09:50.600
as an outsider, in my mind, it seems to me
that once you figure out what you need in

00:09:50.600 --> 00:09:55.080
terms of equipment and you do it, you do it,
now you can replicate it.

00:09:55.080 --> 00:10:00.160
Is there is there some chance to it where
you can do the exact same thing and it only

00:10:00.160 --> 00:10:02.470
works some of the time at this point.

00:10:02.470 --> 00:10:06.829
Speaker 3: So we are on.

00:10:06.829 --> 00:10:09.950
We have just gotten ignition at Lawrence Livermore.

00:10:09.950 --> 00:10:13.100
Arguably, we got it last year, but we don't
need to quibble about all of that.

00:10:13.100 --> 00:10:17.800
What it is, is we're there now, but we're
only just barely getting there.

00:10:17.800 --> 00:10:23.070
And if you've ever played around with something
like it, like doing a science experiment where

00:10:23.070 --> 00:10:27.209
you're just figuring it out, there's often
mistakes and issues and it takes.

00:10:27.209 --> 00:10:30.930
You just kind of have to do something over
and over and over again to learn how to do

00:10:30.930 --> 00:10:32.150
it better.

00:10:32.150 --> 00:10:36.260
But by difficult, what I'm actually getting
at with Fusion is that the conditions needed

00:10:36.260 --> 00:10:41.110
to make a fusion reaction happen are very,
very, very hard to reach and the equipment

00:10:41.110 --> 00:10:46.730
required is very expensive and complicated
when we're comparing to other sources of power,

00:10:46.730 --> 00:10:49.360
like let's just take vision for an easy example.

00:10:49.360 --> 00:10:54.790
You take a rock that we dig out of the ground,
turn it into a metal, stick it into water,

00:10:54.790 --> 00:10:56.700
and the water gets hot.

00:10:56.700 --> 00:11:02.490
For fusion, we have to have some of the largest
lasers in the world hitting little tiny targets

00:11:02.490 --> 00:11:06.910
or massive, incredible magnets that there's
just nothing to compare to.

00:11:06.910 --> 00:11:09.380
Now, that's not to say we can't get good at
that.

00:11:09.380 --> 00:11:12.910
I mean, jet engines used to be one of the
most complicated and incredible things on

00:11:12.910 --> 00:11:15.350
the planet that only militaries could afford.

00:11:15.350 --> 00:11:18.600
And now all of us can ride on a jet aircraft.

00:11:18.600 --> 00:11:22.350
Aircraft themselves used to be something that
almost no one could build.

00:11:22.350 --> 00:11:26.660
But it's we are at like I said, we're kind
of at the Kitty Hawk moment.

00:11:26.660 --> 00:11:28.360
We just got things to go.

00:11:28.360 --> 00:11:32.860
We're not getting seven, four, seven tomorrow
if we have to say they won't happen.

00:11:32.860 --> 00:11:36.709
Speaker 1: If we zoom out a little bit.

00:11:36.709 --> 00:11:43.980
The feeling seems to be growing that in even
as little as 50 years, we're going to look

00:11:43.980 --> 00:11:51.210
at the status quo of cars driving around,
burning gasoline, pulling oil out of the ground,

00:11:51.210 --> 00:11:52.542
burning coal, this to this.

00:11:52.542 --> 00:11:55.560
This is going to seem crazy, for lack of a
better term, right?

00:11:55.560 --> 00:12:01.470
I mean, it seems as though we are that the
forms of energy we're using now are going

00:12:01.470 --> 00:12:05.050
to seem almost primitive compared to what
is apparently on the horizon.

00:12:05.050 --> 00:12:06.120
Is that the right assessment?

00:12:06.120 --> 00:12:08.089
Speaker 3: Yeah, I would say so.

00:12:08.089 --> 00:12:13.260
I mean, we already have a lot of options that
make what's currently powering most of the

00:12:13.260 --> 00:12:14.260
world look primitive.

00:12:14.260 --> 00:12:15.260
Right.

00:12:15.260 --> 00:12:19.040
We can we can already utilize things like
nuclear fission.

00:12:19.040 --> 00:12:23.750
We have, you know, good photovoltaic stuff
like that, the sci fi technologies here, and

00:12:23.750 --> 00:12:24.839
it's only getting better.

00:12:24.839 --> 00:12:33.480
Speaker 1: When people say that fusion power
is the holy grail of clean energy, this is

00:12:33.480 --> 00:12:35.560
based on emissions specifically.

00:12:35.560 --> 00:12:38.010
Or are there other aspects to this to.

00:12:38.010 --> 00:12:41.839
Speaker 3: The claim, the reasoning behind
that.

00:12:41.839 --> 00:12:45.750
And I'm not I'm not entirely sold on that
statement.

00:12:45.750 --> 00:12:47.660
I think it's I've heard it.

00:12:47.660 --> 00:12:48.670
I've heard it plenty of times.

00:12:48.670 --> 00:12:57.649
I think it comes from a kind of science fiction
optimism around fusion and a lack of understanding,

00:12:57.649 --> 00:13:03.130
or maybe not a lack, but a native to take
over some of the other sources.

00:13:03.130 --> 00:13:07.720
Fusion is emissions free, thus the fuel supply
is effectively infinite.

00:13:07.720 --> 00:13:09.610
Like I said, it's it doesn't matter.

00:13:09.610 --> 00:13:10.610
There's so much.

00:13:10.610 --> 00:13:15.389
It's we don't even need to worry about it.

00:13:15.389 --> 00:13:21.279
And those are the big benefits on top of the
lower radioactive waste inventory.

00:13:21.279 --> 00:13:27.320
But what I just described is basically advanced
fission as well.

00:13:27.320 --> 00:13:31.050
And so I think that's kind of where it doesn't
always click.

00:13:31.050 --> 00:13:34.910
It's this idea that if we got to fusion that
all our problems are solved.

00:13:34.910 --> 00:13:36.149
But that's not really true.

00:13:36.149 --> 00:13:39.959
One Like I said, it's going to be hard to
get these things out and cheap and effective

00:13:39.959 --> 00:13:41.899
and reliable.

00:13:41.899 --> 00:13:45.339
But we have options now that can reach a lot
of those goals.

00:13:45.339 --> 00:13:51.899
Fusion, in my mind, is more filling in this
large portfolio of various energy options.

00:13:51.899 --> 00:13:57.470
Each of them have benefits and drawbacks.

00:13:57.470 --> 00:14:00.950
And it's not just this science fiction utopia.

00:14:00.950 --> 00:14:03.329
Speaker 1: Last thing I want to ask you.

00:14:03.329 --> 00:14:04.329
What's the next step?

00:14:04.329 --> 00:14:09.860
Is it just to repeat this very same thing
that happened, or is there some clear advanced

00:14:09.860 --> 00:14:11.440
next step that we go to?

00:14:11.440 --> 00:14:15.640
Speaker 3: So the immediate next step is to
repeat and get more fusion yield.

00:14:15.640 --> 00:14:19.060
We think due to how ignition works, it's like
striking a match.

00:14:19.060 --> 00:14:22.450
Once you can get the match to strike, you
can get more and more fire.

00:14:22.450 --> 00:14:26.820
We think we can get a lot more fusion out
